<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">Teachers&#8217; Corner</h1><p class="ref-lede">Free resources for teaching world history with the Institute: 58 printable worksheets with answer keys on the reading paths and the centuries, and ideas for using the atlas, the timeline and the games in class.</p></header>
<section class="ws-guide-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Printable worksheets</h2><p>Each worksheet prints on a few A4 pages and draws only on the Institute&#8217;s pages: pupils read the reading path or the century page, then put stops or events in order, match people to their dates, explain key terms and answer open questions. The century sheets add a map with numbered states to name. The answer key prints on a page of its own: use <b>Print with the answers</b> on the sheet.</p><h3 class="ws-guide-h">Reading paths <span class="n">25</span></h3><ul class="ws-guide-list"><li><a href="/teachers/path-silk-road">The Silk Road</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-steppe-empires">Empires of the Steppe</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-religion-and-power">Religion and Power</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-empires-of-africa">Empires of Africa</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-age-of-sail">The Age of Sail</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-how-empires-fall">How Empires Fall</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-law-and-government">Law and Government</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-science-and-learning">Science and Learning</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-the-crusades">The Crusades</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-the-fall-of-rome">The Fall of Rome</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-the-americas-before-columbus">The Americas before Columbus</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-india-through-the-ages">India through the Ages</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-china-dynasty-by-dynasty">China, Dynasty by Dynasty</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-the-age-of-revolutions">The Age of Revolutions</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-women-who-ruled">Women Who Ruled</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-city-states-and-leagues">City-States and Leagues</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-great-conquerors">The Great Conquerors</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-explorers-and-travellers">Explorers and Travellers</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-the-great-historians">The Great Historians</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-founders-of-world-religions">Founders of World Religions</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-rebels-against-empire">Rebels against Empire</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-the-great-builders">The Great Builders</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-capitals-of-the-world">Capitals of the World</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-turning-point-battles">Turning-Point Battles</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/path-the-world-wars">The World Wars</a></li></ul><h3 class="ws-guide-h">Centuries <span class="n">33</span></h3><ul class="ws-guide-list ws-guide-cents"><li><a href="/teachers/century-23rd-century-bce">23rd century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-1st-century">1st century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-2nd-century">2nd century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-3rd-century">3rd century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-4th-century">4th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-5th-century">5th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-6th-century">6th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-7th-century">7th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-8th-century">8th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-9th-century">9th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-10th-century">10th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-11th-century">11th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-12th-century">12th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-13th-century">13th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-14th-century">14th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-15th-century">15th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-16th-century">16th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-17th-century">17th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-18th-century">18th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-19th-century">19th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-20th-century">20th century</a></li><li><a href="/teachers/century-21st-century">21st century</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-guide-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Ideas for the classroom</h2><dl class="ws-ideas"><dt><a href="/paths/">Reading paths</a></dt><dd>Twenty-eight guided routes of five to nine stops, each with a line on why it matters. Set a path as reading before a lesson, or give each group a different stop to present.</dd><dt><a href="/atlas">Atlas</a> and <a href="/timeline">World Timeline</a></dt><dd>On a projector, move the atlas&#8217;s year slider through a century and ask what appears and disappears; the timeline shows which states lived at the same time. <a href="/years/">Year pages</a> collect what happened in a single year.</dd><dt><a href="/today-in-history/">Today in History</a></dt><dd>A page for every day of the year: a quick start to a lesson with the anniversaries of the day.</dd><dt><a href="/games">Games</a> and the <a href="/quiz">daily quiz</a></dt><dd>Order the Ages and Which Came First? practise chronology; Globe Hunt practises geography; History Connections asks pupils to group people, events and works by what links them.</dd><dt><a href="/kids">Kids Corner</a> and Kids mode</dt><dd>For younger pupils: the star in the menu bar switches the whole site to larger text and short versions of the long pages, with the hardest stories folded away.</dd><dt><a href="/research-series">Research Series</a> and <a href="/speeches/">Great Speeches</a></dt><dd>For older students: essays that compare empires across five thousand years, and speeches with their setting and, where it is free, the full text.</dd></dl></section>
<section class="ws-guide-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Ages and levels</h2><p>The worksheets suit pupils of about eleven to sixteen. For younger classes, use Kids mode and the <a href="/kids">Kids Corner</a>; older students can follow the references at the end of each profile and the works listed in the essays.</p></section>
<section class="ws-guide-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Citing the Institute and using its pictures</h2><p>Every page offers a ready-made citation (APA, MLA, Chicago and Harvard) under <b>Cite this page</b>. The pictures come from Wikimedia Commons under free licences, with the author and licence under each one: keep that credit when you reuse a picture. The states register and the power curves are free to download from <a href="/dataset">Open Data</a> for projects of your own.</p><p>Spotted a mistake, or have an idea for a worksheet? <a href="/contact?topic=suggest&amp;page=/teachers/">Tell us</a>.</p></section>
